.Dd October 24, 2026
.Dt TRUNCATE 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm truncate
.Nd truncate or extend the length of files
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl c
.Op Fl o
.Fl s Ar size
.Ar file ...
.Nm
.Op Fl c
.Fl r Ar rfile
.Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility adjusts the length of each regular file given on the command-line.
If a
.Ar file
does not exist, it is created, unless the
.Fl c
option is specified.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl c , Fl Fl no-create
Do not create files if they do not exist.
Files that cannot be opened because they do not exist are silently
ignored; the exit value is not affected.
.It Fl o , Fl Fl io-blocks
Treat the
.Ar size
argument as a number of I/O blocks rather than bytes.
The I/O block size of each file is used.
This option requires
.Fl s
and is not valid with
.Fl r
alone.
.It Fl r Ar rfile , Fl Fl reference Ns = Ns Ar rfile
Truncate files to the length of the file
.Ar rfile .
.It Fl s Ar size , Fl Fl size Ns = Ns Ar size
Set or adjust the file size by
.Ar size
bytes (or blocks with
.Fl o ) .
.It Fl Fl help
Display usage information and exit.
.El
.Pp
The long options also accept a separate argument:
.Fl Fl reference Ar rfile
and
.Fl Fl size Ar size
are equivalent to
.Fl Fl reference Ns = Ns Ar rfile
and
.Fl Fl size Ns = Ns Ar size ,
respectively.
.Pp
The
.Ar size
argument is an integer and an optional unit
(example: 10K is 10*1024).
The integer may be omitted when a unit is given, in which case it is
taken as 1.
Units are K, M, G, T, P, E, Z, Y, R, and Q (powers of 1024) or
KB, MB, and so on (powers of 1000).
Binary prefixes can be used, too: KiB=K, MiB=M, and so on.
The lowercase forms
.Cm k ,
.Cm m ,
.Cm g ,
and
.Cm t
are also accepted.
The
.Cm D
suffix is not accepted.
Sizes that do not fit in an
.Vt off_t
are rejected; on systems with a 64-bit
.Vt off_t ,
the Z, Y, R, and Q suffixes overflow for every nonzero value.
.Pp
An absolute
.Ar size
cannot be negative.
.Pp
The
.Ar size
argument may be prefixed by one of the following modifying characters:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm +
extend by
.It Cm -
reduce by
.It Cm <
at most
.It Cm >
at least
.It Cm /
round down to multiple of
.It Cm %
round up to multiple of
.El
.Pp
A leading
.Cm -
is the reduce-by modifier, not a negative absolute size.
.Pp
At least one of
.Fl r
and
.Fl s
must be specified.
When both are given,
the final
.Fl s
argument must begin with a modifying character.
.Pp
If a file is made smaller, its extra data is lost.
If a file is made larger, it will be extended as if by writing bytes
with the value zero.
.Pp
Note that, while truncating a file causes space on disk to be freed,
extending a file does not cause space to be allocated.
To extend a file and actually allocate the space,
it is necessary to explicitly write data to it,
using (for example) the shell's
.Ql >>
redirection syntax, or
.Xr dd 1 .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The
.Nm
utility exits 0 on success and 1 if an error occurred.
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
If the operation fails for an argument,
.Nm
will issue a diagnostic and continue processing the remaining arguments.
.Sh LOCALE
The interface of
.Nm
is U.S. English only, and its output is always UTF-8.
The only supported locale is
.Ev en_US.UTF-8 .
Setting
.Ev LANG ,
.Ev LANGUAGE ,
.Ev LC_ALL ,
.Ev LC_MESSAGES
or any other locale variable has no effect on the interface language,
on the accepted
.Ar size
syntax
.Pq always ASCII digits and the documented suffixes
or on diagnostics.
Legacy encodings such as ISO-8859-1 and Shift-JIS are not supported.
.Sh COMPATIBILITY
Options must precede the file operands.
.Nm
stops option processing at the first non-option argument and does not
permute the command line the way some GNU utilities do; any later
argument that looks like an option is treated as a file operand.
.Pp
The
.Fl Fl version
and
.Fl V
options are not supported.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dd 1 ,
.Xr touch 1 ,
.Xr truncate 2
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Nm
utility conforms to no known standards.
